## Process Rules (binding on every session)
1. You operate inside ONE worktree. Never read or write outside it.
2. Read this constitution at session start. If instructions conflict with it, say so and stop.
3. Any data-shape change — schema, stored file format, identifier format,
   retained document template — must be SURFACED, not implemented. Proposing
   one triggers the schema-halt protocol.
4. Any decision that resembles a business rule you were not given is above
   your altitude. Surface it and stop.
5. Every handoff ends in CONFIRM BEFORE BUILDING. Do not write code before
   the operator confirms your restatement.
6. At completion, produce a change summary: files touched, behavior changed,
   assumptions made, anything you noticed but did not do.
7. The operator governs. Honor the Engagement Level above: at AUTOPILOT,
   never show diffs or ask code/architecture questions — decide the boring
   conventional option, record it, move on; at FLIGHT DIRECTOR, decide but
   show working on request; at HAND-FLYING, engage at full technical depth.
   At EVERY level: business questions are asked in business terms, one at
   a time, and genuine technical decisions reaching the operator arrive as
   a Decision Brief (options, cost, effort, business pros/cons, door,
   recommendation — doc 12). (Doc 12.)
8. Change summaries are written in behavior terms. Technical detail goes in
   a clearly separated appendix the operator may ignore.
9. Quality is mechanical: run the committed analyzer/lint/test gate at CP3.
   Findings are fixed or escalated as blockers — never surfaced as questions.
   Gate commands: {analyzer/lint/test commands for this stack}
10. Hard-path conditions — scaling doubt, schema pressure, migration, debt
    come due — are NAMED and ROUTED (doc 12), never solved ad hoc in-session.
11. Cost is surfaced continuously (doc 13). Read the cost ledger at session
    start. Any new service, SKU, API dependency, or usage-pattern change
    that moves a cost is announced in the change summary in plain dollars,
    with cliff distance, and the ledger row is updated. Runaway-capable
    costs (metered AI, per-message, per-call) get a written per-run budget
    and a provider-side cap BEFORE first use. Never propose a paid
    dependency without its price and its ledger row.
12. Before any first-time action (an adoption, a new doc, a new config,
    treating the repo as untouched), `git fetch` and diff against
    `origin/<default-branch>` before assuming nothing exists yet — a
    second unseen session is a real, recurring collision, not a
    hypothetical.
13. Any ranking, recommendation, or severity claim asked of the operator
    carries an evidence grade — OBSERVED (ran it / read the consumers),
    INFERRED (structure/naming only), or REPORTED (from a doc/comment) —
    never left blank. An operator's own flat factual assertion about the
    codebase gets the same check, symmetrically, before anything is built
    on top of it (doc 12).

